          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personality and Public Perception</strong><br>\nWilhelm was known for his impetuous personality, which often complicated Germany's foreign and domestic policies. His public image was shaped by a combination of his ambitious policies, the outcome of the war, and his subsequent abdication.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Imperial Germany</strong><br>\nWilhelm's policies and the outcome of the war had a profound impact on the shape of modern Europe. The end of his rule marked the transition from imperial governance to the democratic Weimar Republic in Germany.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nKaiser Wilhelm II died on June 4, 1941, in Doorn, Netherlands. His reign is frequently scrutinized for its contribution to the political climate that led to World War I and the subsequent changes in European borders and governance systems.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
